Close the /proc/<pid>/attr/* hidden-type existence oracle. Fallback variant.

Guards the single setattr path: selinux_setprocattr() on 5.10 / 5.15 / 6.1 / 6.6,
and the identical spot inside selinux_lsm_setattr() on 6.12. It does NOT guard
selinux_inode_setxattr(), so `setxattr(p, "security.selinux", ...)` stays
uncovered wherever this variant is the one selected -- use
hide_selinux_attr_6_12.patch where it applies. See common/_hook/README.md.

TWO THINGS WERE FIXED HERE, BOTH OF THEM QUIET ONES

1. The hunk carried three lines of leading context and one of trailing, so GNU
   patch reported fuzz 2 on every tree in the fleet even though every line
   matched -- and fuzz 2 against one line of trailing context means the guard
   was being placed by LINE NUMBER, in the hottest-to-get-wrong function in this
   directory. Context is now balanced at 3/3 and the hunk header names the
   function, so it applies at `-F0` and a misplacement cannot land silently.

2. The match was `strnstr(value, ":ksu:", size)` over the raw write buffer.
   security_context_to_sid() takes the same @size but kmemdup_nul()s it and
   parses it as a C string, so it stops at the first NUL. Writing
   "u:r:untrusted_app:s0\0:ksu:" therefore answered EINVAL here and OK on stock,
   while the same bytes spelled ":kzu:" answered OK on both -- the cloak
   becoming the detector, exactly the inversion this file's own comment warns
   about, arriving through the length argument instead of through placement.
   The scan is now bounded by strnlen(value, size): precisely the bytes the
   parser will look at, and no more.

APPLIES TO

Verified with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` on android12-5.10 (5.10.264),
android13-5.15 (5.15.211), android14-6.1 (6.1.176), android15-6.6 (6.6.142) and
android16-6.12 (6.12.90). security/selinux/hooks.o compiles clean on all five
(ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1), 6.12 included, so the fallback is safe where it is picked.
Generated by editing the real file and running `diff -up`, never by hand.
